\par\par \b \uc2  Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Brazil, Mexico Still Weak \b0\par\par\uc2  By Namju Cho \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1999\par 21:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Mexican peso and Brazilian real late Friday continued to weaken against the dollar, on a mix of negative news in the U.S. and local markets. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso weakened earlier in the session on remarks made by U.S. Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan, in which he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial markets if stock prices were to rise too sharply. The comment was interpreted as yet another sign of monetary policy being tightened to release inflationary pressures, and markets slipped. \par \par \uc2  The Fed raised rates Tuesday by 25 basis points, the second time this year. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's banking leader, Banamex, quoted the Mexican peso at MXN9.375 on Friday, compared with MXN9.353 on Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  At 3:00 p.m., the Dow Jones Industrial Average continued to slide, falling 95 points to about 11102, as did the Standard & Poor's 500 Index, which was down 11 points to about 1350. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, the Brazilian real was also losing against the dollar, mostly on nervousness over domestic political instability and a dose of regional bearishness over Ecuador's woes. \par \par \uc2  At 3:00 p.m. EDT, the Brazilian real was trading at BRR1.940 against the dollar, compared with Thursday's close of BRR1.915. \par \par \uc2  Markets across the region have been unsteady for the past week, as Ecuador said it would defer payment of a $96 million Brady bond payment due Aug. 28. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trying to recover from several \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from hitting BRR2.00 to the dollar, which would have been the lowest level in five months. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold off dollar-linked notes throughout the week to allow market participants to hedge against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings, in turn stemming the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  Markets are expected to be fairly quiet next week ahead of the long Labor Day weekend, one New York-based trader said. He added he'd be looking at U.S. unemployment data for August due out Friday, which would be a barometer for inflation and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Smaller Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also weaker except for the Ecuadorean sucre. \par \par \uc2  The Ecuadorean sucre gained against the dollar after consecutive losses on expectations that President Jamil Mahuad will make an announcement late Friday on the country's ongoing negotiations with the International Monetary Fund to receive $400 million in a standby facility. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Venezuelan bolivar weakened slightly as the government called in the national guard Friday to separate supporters of President Hugo Chavez from protestors. \par \par \uc2  Political tensions in Venezuela are mounting as Friday's move follows the resignation of the Supreme Court President Cecelia Sosa earlier in the week. Sosa resigned in protest after the high court supported the assembly's actions earlier in the week to overhaul the judicial system and possibly fire judges accused of corruption. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about $40 million during the session, higher than average levels of about $25 million, according to local traders. \par \par \uc2  The Colombian peso nearly hit the ceiling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's band, but strengthened slightly before closing, still 2% weaker than Thursday's close at COP1,964. \par \par \uc2  Local traders attributed the slide to "unprovoked speculation" on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Peruvian sol weakened as local banks bought dollars as they expected the sol to slip further after a long holiday weekend, according to a local trader. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Peru will be closed Monday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  Below is a summary of regional exchange rates in late trading compared to one U.S. dollar: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Friday(1845 GMT) Thursday(Close)\par Brazilian Real (BRR)          1.9400          1.9150\par Mexican Peso (MXN)            9.3750*         9.3530\par Venezuelan Bolivar (VEB)    618.7500*       618.2500\par Peruvian Sol (PEN)            3.3755*         3.3690\par Ecuadorean Sucre (ECS)   10,925.0000     11,040.0000\par Colombian Peso (COP)      1,964.0000*     1,923.0000\par Chilean Peso (CLP)          515.5000*       514.2000\par    *Friday's close\par    Note: Argentine Peso is fixed 1:1 against the U.S. dollar under a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board regime.\par -By Namju Cho; 201-938-5692; namju.cho@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 27-08-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1945GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1093 1835 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8r06fc8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
