\par\par \b \uc2  IMF urges tough Brazil budget, 1999 output to ease. \b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1999\par 18:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Aug 23 (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund on Monday praised Brazil's stronger than expected economic performance but urged the government not to ease up in its quest for a wider budget surplus next year. \par \par \uc2  An IMF report summarizing a July 28 review of the Brazilian economy said Brazil's 2000 federal budget should target a primary surplus, excluding debt servicing costs, of 3.25 percent of gross domestic product, up from 3.1 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  "Directors noted that the performance of the Brazilian economy...has been significantly better than projected," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  "For the year as a whole, real GDP is expected to decline by 1 percent or less (compared with the decline of almost 4 percent projected in the revised March program) and consumer price inflation is expected to be contained at around 8 percent." \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian government is trying to persuade a reluctant Congress to approve its cost-cutting 2000 budget. \par \par \uc2  President Fernando Henrique Cardoso, his popularity under threat because of the austerity plans, said on Monday that the economy could grow by at least 4 percent in 2000 if Congress passed the measures. \par \par \uc2  "To lay the foundation for sustained recovery, the authorities' principle challenges are to build on the progress already achieved in implementing fiscal consolidation and to accelerate structural reforms," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  The fund said some directors believed Brazil should aim for an even more ambitious 2000 primary budget surplus than the targeted 3.25 percent. The government needed to be ready to act if the targeted 1999 surplus of 3.1 percent was threatened. \par \par \uc2  The IMF gave no forecasts for Brazilian economic output in 2000, but it urged caution on interest rate reductions. \par \par \uc2  "Consideration of further interest rate reductions should be approached carefully and to ensure the credibility of the new framework, the authorities should stand ready to raise interest rates if expected inflation turns upwards," he said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil has set inflation targets of 8 percent for 1999, 6 percent for 2000 and 4 percent for 2001, with a margin of error of 2 percentage points, but the IMF said some of its directors believed that narrower target bands would be appropriate. \par \par \uc2  Meeting the targets was crucial to ensure the credibility of Brazilian monetary policy, the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said Brazil's banking system had done well to withstand the devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, earlier this year, and to cope with high interest rates of 1998-99. \par \par \uc2  It welcomed \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's flexible exchange rate and limited \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but stressed that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action should be limited to "countering disorderly market conditions." \par \par \uc2  ((Janet Guttsman, Washington newsroom, +1 202 898 8309, washington.economic.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8n023vw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
