\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Ends -2: No  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 1999\par 22:54\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--In illiquid trading Thursday, the Brazilian real (BRR) ended weaker against the dollar, despite a spate of dollar selling in afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  Real-dollar rates, however, will continue to edge up in the sessions ahead, dealers said, noting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday to relieve the pressure. \par \par \uc2  The real closed at a midrate of BRR1.7960 to the dollar on a spread of BRR1.7950-1.7970/dollar, compared with Wednesday's finishing BRR1.7880 per dollar on an equally tight spread. The real opened at BRR1.7950 per dollar and weakened to as low as BRR1.8150 before recovering slightly. \par \par \uc2  Players said the real's afternoon rebound could have been triggered by some banks taking up short dollar positions, or exporters entering the market, but more likely institutions just cashing in on the day's gains. \par \par \uc2  "The market got really expensive, and so the (dollar) selling started," said a trader at Banco Cidade in Sao Paulo. "The market itself forced the real back up," he added, dismissing any notion the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pressured Thursday by Eurobond expirations, depressed local and foreign equity markets, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cautious rate cut Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Falling stock markets and frustration over the new Selic rate only added to the pressure on the real today," said a trader at Banco Boavista in Rio de Janeiro. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday cut Brazil's key lending rate, known as the Selic, to 21% from 22%. The market was looking for something around 20%. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average shed 132 points to end at 10,534.83, while Brazil's benchmark Bovespa Index is off 2.9%, or 342 points, at 11,450. The Bovespa's intraday low was 11,403. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said Banco do Brasil and BBA Creditansdalt were notably active\par in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on the buy side, as was Citibank on the sell side\par in afternoon trading.\par -By Jamie McGeever (5521) 580-9394; jmcgeever@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  06-24-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1654EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv6o013rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
