\par\par \b \uc2  BRAZIL MARKETS WEEK-Real ready to stabilize. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mary Milliken \par \uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 18:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SAO PAULO, June 14 (Reuters) - Brazil's real \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab   is set to settle down in a new range after weakening steadily against the dollar in the first half of the month, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  experts said on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They said they expect the real to trade in a range of 1.73-1.80 for the rest of the month of June after holding to a 1.65-1.75 band between March 30 and June 9.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  At midday Monday, the real was trading at 1.78 to the dollar after closing at an 11-week low of 1.782 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The real is now 32 percent weaker since Brazil abandoned its semi-rigid exchange regime for a free float in mid-January amid heavy capital flight. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slipped 5 centavos thus far in June on maturing Eurobonds and high-yield domestic debt auctioned by government. Around half of the $3.5 billion in foreign obligations coming due in June is estimated to have already passed through the market. \par \par \uc2  "We already new June was going to be an unfavourable month," said Sergio Machado, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Banco Fator in Sao Paulo. "Not only in terms of debt maturities, but also for payments of interest, goods and services." \par \par \uc2  "But the dollar doesn't have to keep rising. At 1.80 reais you should have a strong resistance, while 1.70 becomes a solid support." \par \par \uc2  One development which could put pressure on the real this week is the reintroduction of the financial transactions tax, CPMF, this Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The 0.38 percent CPMF charge on transactions will make financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil more expensive and may spur an initial outflow of capital in search of more tax friendly investment. \par \par \uc2  "But we don't think we will see such a big movement there," said Machado. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks will also be looking at the local stock market for a possible reentry of foreign capital after investment from abroad dropped off sharply in the last weeks. \par \par \uc2  "There could be some inflow of capital," said the head \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Rio de Janeiro bank. "But really it is hard to predict what the flows will be like." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After a 65 percent gain since the beginning of the year, the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's Bovespa Index\tab    has been flat so far in June as fears of a rate rise in the U.S. discourage investment in emerging markets, like Brazil.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But market players say investors seem to have already priced in a 25-50 basis point rise in U.S. rates at the Federal Open Market Committee's next meeting on June 29-30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prospects of a rate hike in the U.S. also have not damped expectations of a fresh rate cut in Brazil's benchmark rate, the Selic, next week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After an encouraging cut in the Selic to 22 percent from 23.5 percent using the bias to ease rates, the market is betting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Monetary Policy Committee (Copom) will chop it to 20-21 percent at its June 23 meeting.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market will also be watching out for any announcement from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) mission now in Brasilia on repealing a limit as to how much \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can spend on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The limit was agreed in March as part of the agreement to secure a second tranche of the $41.5 billion rescue package.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the improvement in the economy and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stability might encourage the IMF to lift the curb, sending a new sign of confidence to the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 55 11 248 5537, mary.milliken@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6e06g4p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
