\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 Central-Bank \highlight\uc2  Head Cuts Benchmark Interest Rate To 32% \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 1999\par 01:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga on Wednesday cut the benchmark Selic interest-rate target to 32% from 34%, citing low inflation and a successful global-bond issue. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said that he decided to trim rates "in light of the successful global-bond issue, perception that (capital) markets are opening for our companies, good rollover rates and excellent news on the inflation front." \par \par \uc2  It was the fourth time Brazil lowered interest rates since late March, after having raised the Selic as high as 45% at the start of March. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said that, in the last three weeks, interbank credit lines are being rolled over at a pace of 150%. He also said credit lines between foreign banks and Brazilian companies have exceeded levels reached at the end of February. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March used $1.5 billion of International Monetary Fund money to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, of the $3 billion earmarked for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that month. \par \par \uc2  The leftover funds boosted the amount destined for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April to $2.5 billion from the $2 billion originally predicted. Fraga declined to reveal how much was spent to prevent the real from weakening so far in April, but said "there were funds left" for May. \par \par \uc2  Fraga did say, however, that May's original $1.5 billion will be boosted by $375 million, the maximum possible. This is because the agreement Brazil inked with the IMF says the country is allowed to carry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leftovers to the following month, equivalent to one-fourth of the total funds allotted for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that period. \par \par \uc2  The IMF lent \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  a total $8 billion exclusively for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through June. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the $3 billion raised from the global-bond issue announced last week will enter Brazil Apr. 30. He added Brazil isn't planning another sovereign debt issue "at this moment." \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv4s02f3m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
