\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Interest Rate/Fraga -3: No Other Issues Planned \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 1999\par 00:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March used $1.5 billion of International Monetary Fund money to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, of the $3 billion earmarked for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that month. \par \par \uc2  The leftover funds boosted the amount destined for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April to $2.5 billion from the $2 billion originally predicted. Fraga declined to reveal how much was spent to prevent the real from weakening so far in April, but said "there were funds left" for May. \par \par \uc2  Fraga did say, however, that May's original $1.5 billion will be boosted by $375 million, the maximum possible. \par \par \uc2  This is because the agreement Brazil inked with the IMF says the country is allowed to carry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leftovers to the following month, equivalent to one-fourth of the total funds allotted for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that period. \par \par \uc2  The IMF lent \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  a total $8 billion exclusively for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through June. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the $3 billion raised from the Global bond issue announced last week will enter Brazil Apr. 30. He added Brazil isn't planning another sovereign debt issue "at this moment." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 28-04-99 \par \par \uc2  2241GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000