\par\par \b \uc2  Brazilian Analysts Say Embattled Real Is Close To Stabilizing \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jamie McGeever, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 1,060 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 21:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil's real is on the cusp of stability after undergoing the most turbulent two months of its nearly five-year history, local analysts said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Revised terms of a $41.5 billion multilateral credit line, significant government strides in its three-year fiscal-adjustment plan and confidence in new \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Arminio Fraga all have helped lift dark clouds of recent market pessimism. \par \par \uc2  Local market sentiment is now betting the real will strengthen to around 1.75 per dollar by the end of June after a dramatic "overshoot" in the wake of January's forced devaluation, according to three Brazil-based analysts consulted by Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  The real's recent performance is providing the best proof of the sudden optimism. At the height of market pessimism earlier this month, the real sank to a historic low of 2.22 to the dollar amid a perceived lack of definition on the policy front. It opened at a midrate of 1.845 to the dollar Tuesday, building further on solid gains posted last week, when it closed at 1.91. \par \par \uc2  Rules governing \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets under last week's reworked economic framework with the International Monetary Fund has particulary pleased market watchers. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dollars to sell, so there will be less speculation (against the real)," predicted Alexandre Azara, chief economist at Bank BBA Creditanstalt in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has vowed to contain volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading - and while it hasn't specified at what points it will do so, it has declared the volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds it has at its disposal through June. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may use up to $3 billion of IMF-led aid in March to maintain stability in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Another $2 billion may be spent in April, with $1.5 billion limits in both May and June. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now has an impressive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and the real will surely not return to over 2.00 to the dollar," predicted Antonio Costa, head of fixed income Oryx Asset Management in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  The IMF is widely tipped to disburse the second tranche of last year's package in April, providing the market with a $9.2 billion liquidity injection to offset still negative capital flows. \par \par \uc2  "This is a very credible policy," said Azara, who likens the IMF aid to a bridge loan. \par \par \uc2  With the near-term funds in place, inflation fears are also easing. A Dow Jones Newswires survey of nine U.S. investment houses in late January pegged 1999 inflation at 28% - with Salomon Smith Barney and Lehman Brothers providing the most bearish estimates, at 35% and a whopping 80%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  President Fernando Henrique Cardoso's government has since set a provisional target of 16.8% inflation for 1999 under the revised IMF targets, and local analysts aren't scoffing. \par \par \uc2  "The inflationary peak has passed, and that peak was when the real was at 2.20 to the dollar," said Leonardo Freire, associate director at Warburg Dillon Read in Rio de Janeiro. \par \par \uc2  Freier, Costa and Azara all described 15% as a reasonable estimate for inflation this year. \par \par \uc2  In February, Brazil's IGP-DI index, a broad measure of wholesale, consumer and construction prices, rose 4.44% from a month earlier. Wholesale prices, which account for 60% of the index, jumped a higher-than-expected 6.99%. \par \par \uc2  But with real-dollar rates on the slide, retailers will be less likely to pass on recent increased production costs to consumers, according to Freire. \par \par \uc2  Costa thinks inflation won't be quite the problem initially feared because of an expected recession. \par \par \uc2  The government is predicting a 3.5% to 4% contraction in gross domestic product this year, after the economy grew a scant 0.15% in 1998. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, financing prospects from other sources than the IMF are also looking brighter. Direct foreign investments this year - as much as $17 billion according to government estimates - are expected to finance Brazil's current account deficit, allowing companies to tap trade finance lines to kick-start exports. \par \par \uc2  While the government's goal of replacing last year's $6 billion trade deficit with an $11 billion surplus in 1999 is considered overly optimistic by some observers, Freire, Costa and Azara see a surplus of $8 billion to $10 billion as attainable. \par \par \uc2  The government's 1999 deficit target on the current account front is 3% of GDP, down from 4.95% for the most recent 12 months ended January. \par \par \uc2  In another positive development, leading commercial bankers in Europe, the U.S. and Japan have pledged to maintain February's trade and interbank lines through August after Finance Minister Pedro Malan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Fraga lobbied for such guarantees in a global roadshow that began last week. \par \par \uc2  Once harvest exports start rolling in May and an estimated $5.2 billion in foreign debt obligations over the next two months are met, analysts say real-dollar rates will begin to settle. That, in turn, should allow the country to tap international capital markets once again. \par \par \uc2  Citibank Vice Chairman William Rhodes said Monday he was "very optimistic" Brazil would be able to access international markets before the end of April - not bad, considering Brazil's early bungled steps toward a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-January. \par \par \uc2  "If trade finance lines return, a sovereign government bond issue would be the benchmark for the private sector to enter the Eurobond market again, brightening the mood even more," said Oryx's Costa, noting the near-completion of the government's fiscal-adjustment plan. \par \par \uc2  The last piece in the policy jigsaw puzzle will be final Congressional approval to extend and increase the CPMF financial-transactions tax, which could come as soon as this Wednesday after several months of delays. \par \par \uc2  The government hopes the entire adjustment package will save 28 billion reals this year - and serve notice that Brazil is at last serious about fixing the chronic state of its public accounts. The government posted a nominal deficit of 72.38 billion reals - or 8.02% of gross domestic product - at the end of 1998. \par \par \uc2  Jamie McGeever; jmcgeever@ap.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv3g023kr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
