\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's New  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Funds Seen Bridging Gap \b0\par\par\uc2  By Adriana Arai and Jamie McGeever, Staff Reporters \par \uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 16:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- The $8 billion the International Monetary Fund has granted Brazil to prop up its real from now through June should bridge the country's current account gap until foreign capital inflows return to normal, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  According to the revised terms of an IMF-led credit line, Brazil will have $3 billion available to inject in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in March, another $2 billion in April, $1.5 billion in May, and $1.5 billion in June. \par \par \uc2  "The $8 billion are largely enough," said Octavio de Barros, chief economist with Banco Bilbao Vizcaya in Sao Paulo, adding that the funds are "very well distributed" over the four months. \par \par \uc2  The funds are seen as essential. \par \par \uc2  "This amount corresponds to the lack of liquidity for balance-of-payments financing until June," said Marcelo Allain, chief economist for BMC bank in Sao Paulo, referring to bonds and amortizations due in coming months. "Foreign banks won't allow these (debts) to be rolled over, as they're restraining their exposure to Brazil." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy director Luiz Fernando Figueiredo said Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  may actually spend more than the $8 billion specified in the IMF pact, noting that it depends on whether Brazil is able to boost its international reserves, local daily O Estado de Sao Paulo reported Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "New money that is added to reserves may be used in the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Figueiredo was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, a day after the new IMF terms and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures were announced, the real (BRR) strengthened to BRR1.88 per dollar from Monday's close of BRR1.98/dlr. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened trading Wednesday at BRR1.87/dlr. Before the free-float in mid-January, the real was quoted at BRR1.21. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also welcomed the disclosure Monday of the economic framework for the $41.5-billion aid package. The new agreement with the IMF made public all the macroeconomic variables used in the model and expected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quotes against the dollar each month. \par \par \uc2  "We had zero idea of what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's parameters for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were before," said Christian Stracke, economist at BT Alex Brown in New York. \par \par \uc2  For Barros, the government introduced a little "predictability" in the performance of international reserves and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, essential for private-sector decision-making. \par \par \uc2  "And most importantly, the government didn't water down the free-float regime," Barros added. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry's economic policy secretary Amaury Bier said Tuesday that there will be two types of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  One, with the IMF's $8 billion, aims to cover Brazil's current account deficit during four months of an expected sharp shortage of dollars. The other, Bier said, has no pre-set limits and is a type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to control excess volatility, using what is left of Brazil's reserves - at $34.572 billion Monday. \par \par \uc2  According to calculations by BBV's Barros, Brazil's public- and private-sector foreign commitments tally up to $4.385 billion in March. That figure includes bonds coming due, amortizations, interest, repatriation of profits, and other loans. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think Brazil will have to use all the $3 billion this month," he said, estimating there will be around $1 billion a month in foreign direct investment and 10% to 20% of debt rollover. It's "too naive" to think that "dollar inflows have dried completely," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The margin is comfortable, and Brazil may save some of March's munition for April, when the pressure on the real will be bigger," he said, without revealing his calculations for next month. \par \par \uc2  To restore dollar inflows, the government is counting on the return of foreign credit lines to Brazilian companies and a sharp turnaround in its trade balance - to an $11 billion surplus in 1999 from a $8.4 billion deficit last year. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts think the $11 billion surplus goal is overly ambitious, but they agree exports will pick up fast, becoming the short-term supplier of dollars in the market aside from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In February, Brazil posted its first real trade surplus, totaling $219 million, since May 1996 - real because in June 1998 technical factors generated a small surplus. \par \par \uc2  Barros estimated that the trade surplus will stand around $400 million in March, $700 million in April, and will surpass the $1 billion mark in May. \par \par \uc2  "Until the end of the year, exports should outpace imports by $7.9 billion," he said. \par \par \uc2  BMC's Allain is more optimistic about the second half of the year, when he believes the supply and demand for dollars will even out. \par \par \uc2  "There may be four, five months which may not be so positive, when foreign debt commitments must be met," he said. "But in the second half, an improving trade balance and lower debt burden will lead to a more balanced \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As a result of the improved trade balance figures, Brazil's current\par account deficit is expected to shrink to 3%-3.3% of GDP in 1999,\par according to analysts, from last year's 4.5%.\par    -By Adriana Arai and Jamie McGeever; 5561-321-1224; aarai@ap.org\par    (Michelle Wallin in Buenos Aires contributed to this article.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1833 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv3a0233y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
