\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-New IMF deal gives Brazil real a reprieve. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Noriko Yamaguchi \par \uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 21:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, March 9 (Reuters) - The new IMF deal should buoy Brazil's embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first half of 1999, but some overly confident forecasts on the rest of the year may leave the real struggling again, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a boost after new terms set in the International Monetary Fund's rescue plan allowed the government to spend more dollars to defend the real. Brazil's Finance Ministry announced Monday that the new rescue plan authorises the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend up to $8 billion by June to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Those precious dollars will come from \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves, currently at $34.8 billion, and the $9 billion second tranche of a $41.5 billion financial bailout package, expected to be freed up in April. \par \par \uc2  Markets parched of dollars welcomed the additional cash and analysts said that, in the short term at least, it would help ease pressure from foreign corporate bonds, with maturities concentrated in the first half of this year. \par \par \uc2  In a first sign of relief, the real jumped 3.7 percent to 1.91 reais per dollar by midday Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The measure will at least serve as a bridge for a market suffering from a huge demand for dollars," said Marcelo Allain, chief economist at BMC Bank in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  Allain said about $6.5 billion in Eurobonds were expected to come due between March and June this year. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , under the terms laid out by the IMF, was allowed to spend $3 billion in March, $2 billion in April, $1.5 billion in May and another $1.5 billion in June to "water markets" in which the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen drying up. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the amount is sufficient to keep the real stable in the first half, as it just about covers the needs of Brazilian companies paying off debt - the main source of market turbulence at the moment. \par \par \uc2  But with no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels set for the second half, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real will have to rely on fundamental progress in cutting its budget deficit and taming the high inflation expected this year. \par \par \uc2  The IMF was due to review Brazil's progress in the future but it was not clear whether new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  targets would be set, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to meet its new target of a primary budget surplus, excluding interest payments, of 3.1 percent of gross domestic product, up from an original 2.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the government still faces a big challenge in getting the rest of its fiscal austerity measures through Congress. \par \par \uc2  First on the political agenda was the government's battle to get a key financial transactions tax known as the CPMF through Congress. Voting was set to resume this week. \par \par \uc2  "President Fernando Henrique Cardoso has more to do on the fiscal front," Allain said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in particular doubted the government would be able to control inflation, after the IMF forecast Brazil would end 1999 with an inflation of 16.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  "It's a 'show me' story. We have to see that inflation comes down, but there is absolutely no guarantee," said Geoffrey Dennis, global emerging market strategist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Dennis noted that Brazil's benchmark inflation index Fipe, which measures consumer prices in industrial Sao Paulo, was already up 1.4 percent in February, translating into annualized inflation of 18 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Brazil-IMF accord also forecast the real at 1.7 per dollar by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  But for the fragile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to settle around that level without strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , markets would have to receive an influx of export revenues, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The 1999 trade surplus target in the IMF agreement is way too optimistic at $11 billion, analysts said. In 1998, Brazil posted a $6.4 billion trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  "The measure foresees the trade balance turning into a surplus in the second half, with fewer imports and more exports. But I think the surplus will only reach $6 billion..." Allain said. "It would be hard to get a surplus of $11 billion." \par \par \uc2  Both Allain and Dennis said Brazil had a long way to go to regain investor confidence and calm \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "Brazilians have given themselves the biggest possible chance yet of stabilising markets and moving towards key targets," said Deutsche Bank's Dennis. \par \par \uc2  In the meantime, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga and Finance Minister Pedro Malan were due to travel separately to New York, London, Frankfurt, Paris and Tokyo this week for talks with private banks and authorities to help restore credit flows to Latin America's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 55 11 248 5423, saopaulo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv39072by\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
