\par\par \b \uc2  Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Brazil Sparking New Optimism \b0\par\par\uc2  By Enza Tedesco \par \uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 20:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were strengthening against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday, underpinned by renewed optimism after Monday's release of a new fiscal accord between the International Monetary Fund and Brazil, \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, trading of the Ecuadorean sucre was still halted as the government extended a surprise banking holiday to a second day Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  At 1845 GMT, New York-based traders quoted the Brazilian real at BRR1.91 per dollar on a spread of BRR1.90-BRR1.92, stronger from Monday's close of BRR1.98/dollar. \par \par \uc2  With New York and Brazilian traders hailing Brazil's new targets for the next three years, traders in the U.S. said they expect the real to stabilize in the short term. \par \par \uc2  "It's definitely a good start, and the real will likely trade at these levels this week," a trader in New York said. "But it's now up to Congress to keep the good mood alive and make targets attainable." \par \par \uc2  Among the IMF-sponsored targets is a year-end exchange rate of BRR1.70/dlr, or a 29% depreciation from Jan. 13, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first widened the real's trading band. The real sank to an intraday low of BRR2.22/dlr at its weakest point last week. \par \par \uc2  Under the new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with a maximum of $8 billion until the end of June, when these levels will be reviewed with the IMF once again. \par \par \uc2  "It's very positive news, because now the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a cap and it won't just waste its reserves and the IMF money in defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a trader in New York said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile in Mexico, the peso has gained ground for its fifth straight session, despite coming off slightly from its opening levels. At 1845 GMT, the peso was changing hands at MXN9.7990 per dollar from Monday's close of 9.8050. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strengthened 8.3% since Jan. 14, the day before the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the real to float. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's relatively strong economy, links to the U.S. and high interest rates are providing the backdrop for the peso's strength. Both Mexican and New York-based traders said, however, they expected rates on the bellwether 28-day Treasury bills, or Cetes, to drop 0.5 to 1.0 percentage point at Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction from last week's 26.78%. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 09-03-99 \par \par \uc2  1919GMT \par \par \uc2  1093 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3901uxk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
