\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 -IMF deal limits Cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  - gov't. \b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, March 8 (Reuters) - A revised rescue deal put together by the International Monetary Fund for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  includes limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a letter of intent between the two sides released by the Finance Ministry on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Under the agreement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may spend up to $3 billion in March1, $2 billion in April and $1.5 billion in May and again in June. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also limited to spending 30 percent of its monthly maximum in any one week, the document said. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets have been waiting for details on how \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend the real which has slumped by nearly 40 percent since the country was forced to float the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-January. \par \par \uc2  Further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits for the second half of 1999 would be set at a future review of Brazil's agreement with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The letter of intent also spelled out macroeconomic forecasts for Brazil's economy with net public debt expected to rise sharply to 49.3 percent of GDP by the end of the year. In November, the ratio stood at 41.9 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's economy was seen shrinking by between 3.5 and 4 percent this year, but would recover in 2000 to grow by between 3.5 percent and 4 percent and to between 4.5 and 5 percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's trade balance, which went $6.43 billion into the red in 1998, would post a huge surplus of $11 billion in 1999 and the current account deficit, 4.48 percent of GDP in 1998, would fall to about 3 percent this year, the document said. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark overnight Selic interest rate would also fall to an average 28.8 percent in 1999, down from 45 percent now, the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would end the year at a forecast 1.70 to the dollar and inflation for the year would be 16.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Brasilia newsroom 5561 314 1193; brasilia.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv3800t7s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
