\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil real ends up on dlr inflows, Fraga comments. \b0\par\par\uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 22:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, March 4 (Reuters) - Brazil's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended 3.9 percent firmer at 2.07 per dollar Thursday, helped by dollar inflows and comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new president Arminio Fraga which hinted at more support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The real began firming in late afternoon trade after market players started digesting Fraga's comments and the decision by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it will raise the market's benchmark interest rate Selic to 45 percent from 39 percent, effective on Friday, until April 14, with a bias toward lower rates for the period. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers cheered the announcement, saying higher interest rates, at least for the time being, would support the real. \par \par \uc2  Traders also liked Fraga's comment hinting the government was ready to save the real from weakening too much, they said. \par \par \uc2  Fraga, in a news conference given after the announcement of the interest rate hike, said Brazil may use part of the money extended by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) to "attend dollar needs" in the country. \par \par \uc2  "Players interpreted that comment as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would use reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets," said one trader at Banco Fator. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real, at 2.07 per dollar, has lost 41.5 percent of its value since the government relaxed its rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on January 13. \par \par \uc2  In the interest rate futures market, investors projected rates at 42.62 percent by the end of March, down from a projection of 45.02 percent on Wednesday. Rates were seen at 49.41 percent at the end of April, down from a projection of 53.88 percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said rates were seen falling as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  highlighted there was a negative bias on the new Selic rate of 45 percent, indicating that rates were expected to fall. \par \par \uc2  ((Sao Paulo newsroom, 55 11 248 5423, noriko.yamaguchi@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv34022iw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
