\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Brazil's Fraga to clear final Senate vote. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Joelle Diderich \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, March 3 (Reuters) - Brazil's Senate was expected to confirm economist Arminio Fraga as the new president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, ending weeks of uncertainty which have seen the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spiral to a record low. \par \par \uc2  Economists said markets would heave a sigh of relief at the confirmation of Fraga and five new \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directors, a team of former market operators handpicked to steer Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a turbulent post-devaluation period. \par \par \uc2  Fraga's first task will be to send markets a clear signal of how the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to keep a lid on inflation after the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, skidded to a new closing low of 2.17 per dollar Tuesday, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "He's really competent in explaining his ideas to the market," said Felipe Garcia, economist at I.D.E.A. in New York. "Everyone wants him to take over as soon as possible, but it's taking these days and these days have been very volatile," \par \par \uc2  Investors have been nervously twiddling their thumbs since Fraga was named over a month ago, as Senate delayed his confirmation while lawmakers took off for Carnival holidays and then installed new committees following elections last year. \par \par \uc2  The U.S.-educated Fraga was working as a top aide to billionaire speculator George Soros when he was invited to become Brazil's third \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief this year. \par \par \uc2  A Senate economic panel Friday approved his nomination, due to be sealed in a full vote on the Senate floor Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  With this hurdle cleared, economists said they were anxious to see the inflation target Brazil was said to be discussing with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) as part of talks aimed at securing the release of more international loans. \par \par \uc2  They also want a clear set of rules for \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, after a recent round of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  left investors speculating whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  was defending a set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can either \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically to ease swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, or adopt a system similar to Mexico's, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  automatically steps in if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuates by more than a set percentage level. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's going to be a clear rule of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Garcia, referring to the latter system. \par \par \uc2  The real has lost 44 percent of its value against the dollar since Brazil was forced to abandon its strict \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls on Jan. 13. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved Tuesday to tighten liquidity and ease pressure on the real, which has weakened as the market is short of dollars while companies still have to honour some $2 billion in corporate Eurobond payments due this month. \par \par \uc2  Fraga will have no time to settle into his office - on his first day on the job Thursday, he is due to preside a meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee (Copom). \par \par \uc2  Some economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should jack up interest rates, already towering at an annualized 41 percent, despite fears this would increase the chances of Brazil defaulting on its mountain of domestic debt. \par \par \uc2  "That would be a signal of a very tough and determined policy," said Neil Dougall, chief Latin America economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in London. "Debt default is not triggered by a 3 or 4 percent rise in interest rates." \par \par \uc2  Dougall noted that Mexico and South Korea both saw a surge in inflation following devaluations, meaning real interest rates turned negative for a short while. \par \par \uc2  Economists calculate Brazil's effective benchmark overnight rate stood at 2.38 percent in February. This meant real rates of return were negative last month, with inflation hitting 3.61 percent, according to the private Fundacao Getulio Vargas. \par \par \uc2  Dougall said a rate hike, in the context of an expected agreement with the IMF and improving trade figures, would encourage risk-averse investors to keep their precious dollars in Brazil and prevent a further overshooting of the real. \par \par \uc2  "You've got in place the factors which will prevent this overshooting," said Dougall. "In the meantime, maybe it's worth sending a strong signal from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it's not going to accommodate inflation expectations." \par \par \uc2  ((-Brasilia newsroom 5561 314-1193, fax 5561 223-5918)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv3303f7m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
