\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Fraga Sees Free-Float As Best Option For Embattled Real \b0\par\par\uc2 1,442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 1999\par 18:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -(Dow Jones)- Arminio Fraga, in line to head Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a firestorm of volatility in Latin America's largest economy, Friday said the free-float \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is the nation's best option as it tries to emerge from a crippling recession. \par \par \uc2  Fraga, a former adviser to financier George Soros, spoke before the Senate's economics affairs committee endorsed his nomination for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  presidency, as expected. He said he will use monetary policy as his primary weapon to manage the exchange rate - which analysts said is likely to mean high rates until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is comfortable with the inflation outlook and the level of its embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real. \par \par \uc2  Fraga told lawmakers the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must focus almost exclusively on maintaining a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and controlling inflation, and said he considered free-float \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems "viable and sustainable." Although he said the government wants to allow for a "responsible" cut in Brazil's sky-high rates, he indicated a strict monetary policy will be crucial for Brazil to adapt to its new free-float regime. \par \par \uc2  The remarks amounted to his first public statements on monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate policy since being nominated Feb. 2 to replace Francisco Lopes. Lopes was sacked earlier this month following the decision to devalue and then float the real in the face of an investor exodus in January. \par \par \uc2  The real, which stabilized somewhat after it was freed to trade at market levels against the dollar, came under strain again this week amid renewed worries about Brazil's weak fiscal condition, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has returned repeatedly to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a dollar seller to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were the first since \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandonded the real's widened trading band and let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float freely on Jan. 15. \par \par \uc2  "The preservation of the buying power of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be the most important task of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Fraga told lawmakers Friday. \par \par \uc2  In endorsing Brazil's move to a floating exchange rate, Fraga said international experience had shown that the free-float is most successful over the long run when it is closest to "pure" - that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  only in exceptional cases and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supported by underlying policies. \par \par \uc2  The government is now trying to enact fiscal-policy reforms that it hopes will win back investors, but its deficit-cutting drive remains mired in Congress and complicated by new revenue expectations linked to the plunge in the real. The government has also been working with the International Monetary Fund to readjust its fiscal program in hopes of receiving continued aid under a $41 billion rescue package arranged last November. \par \par \uc2  Investors began heading for the exits in droves last month over worries about Brazil's ability to cut budget deficits and meet debt obligations. After a lull, the stampede picked up again this week. \par \par \uc2  After a third straight day of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, the real closed at 2.04 reals to the dollar, unchanged from Wednesday. Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to 2.0350 reals to the dollar, amid unconfirmed rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have spent little of its precious dollar war chest: The country's hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at nearly $36 billion yesterday, little changed from a month ago. \par \par \uc2  In all, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 41% of its dollar value since the country's first move to devalue on Jan. 13. On Tuesday, the real was trading safely below two reals to the dollar before it suddenly weakened to 2.09 reals to the dollar, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fraga, who still must be approved by the full Senate Wednesday, said he intends to maintain a strict monetary stance. But at the same time, he said Brazilian interest rates must come down to international levels and that he sees room for a short-term decline. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  benchmark rate currently stands at 39%, a level designed to attract foreign investors to the weakened real. But Brazil's high interest rates have aggravated the economy's slide into recession. \par \par \uc2  On inflation, Fraga said Brazil can't return to the days of hyperinflation, which was stamped out by the Cardoso government's tight monetray policies in the mid-1990s. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nominee avoided offering an annual inflation rate estimate for 1999, but he forecast single-digit annual inflation in the year 2000, which should drop below 5% in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said he hopes to stabilize the relationship between public deficit and gross domestic product. Brazil's public deficit was equivalent to about 8% of GDP in 1998. "The government has total awareness of the dangers of not controlling the public deficit," Fraga said. The fiscal plan the government is trying to push through Congress aims to save more than 28 billion reals in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Fraga noted that Brazil has lost international credibility over the last 18 months, and will face a difficult first half of 1999, but said he sees a recovery in the final half of the year. \par \par \uc2  Throughout the committee session, questions and accusations returned to Fraga's last job: a New York-based managing director, in charge of emerging markets portfolio management and strategies, for Soros Fund Management LLC. \par \par \uc2  In essence, some senators accused Fraga of being in cahoots with the international speculators who they blame for Brazil's current economic situation: a devastated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possible double-digit inflation, and a growing recession. \par \par \uc2  "I can say that I never did anything that could have harmed my country," he responded. "I am here to serve my country as a technocrat and as an economist." \par \par \uc2  Fraga declined comment on the type of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy that might be adopted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the real. Brazilian officials have said in recent weeks that the government is discussing possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules with the IMF as part of the review of the aid package. The IMF said Friday that the revised accord with Brazil will be unveiled next week, in which the Brazilian government will make new committments to a number of economic targets in return for continued funding under the IMF-organized loan package. \par \par \uc2  Negative sentiment in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had been effectively neutralized during recent Carnival celebrations, but pessimism is on the rise as President Fernando Henrique Cardoso's administration struggles to find a solution to the continuing economic crisis, which many fear could eventually spread throughout Latin America, and in turn weaken the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Cardoso met Friday with state governors, seven of whom are pushing for renegotiation of their debts to the central government, something Cardoso has ruled out. Following the meeting, the Estado news agency reported federal and state officials as saying the Brazilian government will make some concessions to states with financial difficulties but won't revise the terms of existing debt contracts. \par \par \uc2  Itamar Franco, the governor of Brazil's third-largest state, helped reignite the capital flight last month when he declared a 90-day moratorium on his state's federal debt payments. Franco, who boycotted Friday's meeting of governors, has grown more bellicose in his opposition to Cardoso - all of which have contributed to worries about the government's ability to put Brazil's fiscal house in order. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the finance ministry, hoping to avoid a default on debt owed it by Brazilian towns and cities, said it has agreed to refinance municipal debts of 17.4 billion reals, with the debts to be paid over a period of 30 years at 9% annual interest rates. The government had earlier discarded the possibility of such a restructuring, but National Treasury Secretary Eduardo Guimaraes said the new proposal makes the payment of municipal debts viable. "It's unrealistic to think that they (municipalities) would be able to pay these debts on their own," he said. \par \par \uc2  If the federal government hadn't proposed rolling over the debts, the city of Sao Paulo, Latin America's largest, would have defaulted on 410 million reals worth of municipal bonds due March 1, according to authorities. \par \par \uc2  "Under the current circumstances, a default by Sao Paulo would have been a market disaster," said consultant Raul Velloso, a former government advisor. \par \par \uc2  "It ends the threat of a second Itamar effect," said the head trader at a Sao Paulo bank, referring to Minas Gerais governor Franco's moratorium on federal debt payments. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv2q01zgp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
